entry acute-radiation-syndrome
term: Acute radiation syndrome
related_terms: sievert
related_terms: dose-rate
related_terms: absorbed-dose
quick_facts: Needs a very large dose in a short time
quick_facts: The threshold is about 1 sievert, or 1000 millisieverts
quick_facts: Symptoms such as nausea and vomiting can start within hours

sources:
    {"organization":"U.S. Environmental Protection Agency","title":"Radiation Health Effects","url":"https://www.epa.gov/radiation/radiation-health-effects"}
    {"organization":"World Health Organization","title":"Ionizing radiation and health effects","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/ionizing-radiation-and-health-effects"}

definition:
    Acute radiation syndrome is the illness caused by a very high radiation exposure delivered over a short period of time. The dose threshold is about 1 sievert (1000 millisieverts). It can cause symptoms such as nausea and vomiting within hours. The everyday doses people receive are far below it.

debunking_snippet:
    “Any radiation exposure leads to radiation sickness.” Exposure to the low levels of radiation in the environment does not cause immediate health effects. Acute radiation syndrome needs about 1000 millisieverts in a short time.
